<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1080–1169</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Hamid al-Gharnati was an Andalusian Muslim traveller born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> who journeyed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the twelfth century. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Saqsin on the lower Volga and travelled on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and his writings describe the peoples, trade and marvels of these lands. His accounts are a valuable source for the Volga region before the Mongol conquest.</p></div>
